1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:00,908
Hi, everyone.

2
00:00:01,440 --> 00:00:02,696
Thanks for joining me today.

3
00:00:02,747 --> 00:00:04,318
I'm Praveen Kumar Gopalakrishnan.

4
00:00:04,926 --> 00:00:07,177
I'm a senior technical
architect at Salesforce.

5
00:00:07,945 --> 00:00:12,081
So in my work with API-driven
systems and enterprise architecture,

6
00:00:12,778 --> 00:00:16,802
one thing we always think about is
how to keep systems reliable and

7
00:00:16,802 --> 00:00:18,833
available even when things go wrong.

8
00:00:19,580 --> 00:00:23,389
But there is one situation where I
think we need to look at recovery a

9
00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:25,381
little differently, a cyber incident.

10
00:00:25,985 --> 00:00:26,530
Think about it.

11
00:00:27,039 --> 00:00:30,169
When a system goes down, our
first instinct is to bring it

12
00:00:30,181 --> 00:00:31,801
back as quickly as possible.

13
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:33,795
That's usually the right thing to do.

14
00:00:34,451 --> 00:00:38,584
But what happens when the system
went down because it was compromised?

15
00:00:38,908 --> 00:00:41,976
Do we really want to bring everything
back exactly the way it was?

16
00:00:42,521 --> 00:00:45,408
Because doing that might also
bring back the same weaknesses

17
00:00:45,471 --> 00:00:46,610
that allowed the attacker.

18
00:00:47,598 --> 00:00:52,203
And that's the idea behind today's
topic, your backup is not your recovery.

19
00:00:52,933 --> 00:00:57,148
I'll share a practical approach to
using that recovery window to rebuild

20
00:00:57,168 --> 00:01:03,644
trust in our APIs without rebuilding the
same security weaknesses For a normal,

21
00:01:03,694 --> 00:01:09,352
infrastructure failure, restoring the last
known good state is often exactly right.

22
00:01:10,019 --> 00:01:13,517
A failed deployment or a dead
server doesn't usually change

23
00:01:13,546 --> 00:01:15,063
who can access your APIs.

24
00:01:15,593 --> 00:01:17,244
A cyber incident is different.

25
00:01:17,926 --> 00:01:22,998
The attacker, the attacker may have used
a long-lived service credential, a service

26
00:01:22,999 --> 00:01:28,040
account with too much permission, or
a network path that was trusted simply

27
00:01:28,044 --> 00:01:30,008
because it was inside the environment.

28
00:01:30,762 --> 00:01:34,776
A backup can preserve all of that,
so the data may be back, but the

29
00:01:34,776 --> 00:01:36,564
trust blo- problem is still there.

30
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:42,606
Those are two different recovery jobs
Look at the payment example on the right.

31
00:01:43,505 --> 00:01:47,194
Checkout calls the payment API, and
the service can reach four other

32
00:01:47,195 --> 00:01:51,998
places: the ledger, customer profile,
fraud scoring, and notifications.

33
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:57,766
The important point is that one
compromised identity can affect more

34
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:00,050
than the service where it was first used.

35
00:02:00,829 --> 00:02:03,548
It may also reach financial
records or customer data.

36
00:02:04,421 --> 00:02:07,895
So when the team restores the
payment API, we cannot just check

37
00:02:07,914 --> 00:02:09,577
whether it starts successfully.

38
00:02:10,018 --> 00:02:12,820
We need to understand
which paths are still open.

39
00:02:13,549 --> 00:02:18,185
We'll build this into a more useful
dependency graph in a moment There

40
00:02:18,185 --> 00:02:19,931
is already good guidance to build on.

41
00:02:20,349 --> 00:02:24,865
NIST 800-207 gives us the zero
trust architecture foundations.

42
00:02:25,495 --> 00:02:30,649
207A brings identity and policy
enforcement into cloud native services.

43
00:02:31,418 --> 00:02:37,131
184 is focused on cybersecurity
event recovery, and 61 covers

44
00:02:37,201 --> 00:02:40,291
incident response as part of the
wider risk management life cycle.

45
00:02:41,060 --> 00:02:44,395
I'm not suggesting those
publications are missing the basics.

46
00:02:44,424 --> 00:02:48,784
What I'm proposing is a practical
way to connect them for API recovery.

47
00:02:49,591 --> 00:02:50,401
What do we map?

48
00:02:50,629 --> 00:02:51,521
What do we revoke?

49
00:02:51,834 --> 00:02:54,992
Where do we enforce, and how do
we know it is safe to restore?

50
00:02:55,992 --> 00:02:57,715
Here's the path I want to walk through.

51
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:01,514
We map the API relationships
first, so we know what the

52
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:03,704
compromised identity could reach.

53
00:03:04,126 --> 00:03:09,090
Then we contain that identity and deal
with access that may already be in flight.

54
00:03:09,848 --> 00:03:13,930
Next, we put identity and policy
checks in the request path.

55
00:03:14,471 --> 00:03:18,187
When trust is uncertain, we do
not have to treat every request

56
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:19,998
as simply safe or unsafe.

57
00:03:20,773 --> 00:03:22,454
We can reduce what it is allowed to do.

58
00:03:23,352 --> 00:03:27,188
And finally, we modernize in risk
order instead of trying to rewrite

59
00:03:27,245 --> 00:03:28,908
everything during an incident.

60
00:03:29,925 --> 00:03:34,335
I'll use one illustrative payment,
platform incident throughout, so

61
00:03:34,335 --> 00:03:39,403
we can see how each step connects
to the next The first job is

62
00:03:39,403 --> 00:03:41,045
to understand the blast radius.

63
00:03:41,154 --> 00:03:44,657
So for every service relationship,
ask seven simple questions.

64
00:03:45,227 --> 00:03:46,767
What can this identity do?

65
00:03:47,097 --> 00:03:48,699
How important is the service?

66
00:03:48,979 --> 00:03:51,659
How close is it to the
compromised environment?

67
00:03:52,383 --> 00:03:53,835
How does it authenticate?

68
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:55,569
What data does it touch?

69
00:03:55,712 --> 00:03:57,244
Are there hidden trust paths?

70
00:03:57,593 --> 00:04:00,122
And how often does the
relationship change?

71
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:03,563
Take two services in a
pa-- a payment platform.

72
00:04:04,339 --> 00:04:08,627
The ledger API can write financial
records, so its privilege and

73
00:04:08,627 --> 00:04:10,408
business impact are both high.

74
00:04:11,145 --> 00:04:14,988
The notification service can send
payment updates, but it does not

75
00:04:15,014 --> 00:04:16,623
need permission to change the ledger.

76
00:04:17,462 --> 00:04:21,176
Those two connections should not
receive the same level of access.

77
00:04:21,423 --> 00:04:25,580
that is why we map the details
before bringing services back.

78
00:04:26,580 --> 00:04:29,690
This is the graph I want
the response team to build.

79
00:04:29,964 --> 00:04:32,387
The checkout service
calls the payment API.

80
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:34,925
The payment API then reaches four places.

81
00:04:35,287 --> 00:04:39,282
It writes to the ledger, reads
customer details, checks the fraud

82
00:04:39,282 --> 00:04:40,979
service, and sends a notification.

83
00:04:41,690 --> 00:04:46,229
For example, the identity in the middle
is the one we believe was compromised.

84
00:04:46,939 --> 00:04:48,569
Notice what the graph gives us.

85
00:04:48,843 --> 00:04:52,113
Not just a list of systems,
but the direction of access

86
00:04:52,149 --> 00:04:53,256
and the kind of operation.

87
00:04:54,083 --> 00:04:59,089
If the attacker has that identity, the
ledger write path is an immediate concern.

88
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:01,697
Customer data is another concern.

89
00:05:01,771 --> 00:05:06,556
The notification path may be lower impact,
but it still belongs in the investigation.

90
00:05:07,542 --> 00:05:10,915
We can assemble this picture
from gateway logs, network logs,

91
00:05:11,019 --> 00:05:14,538
configuration, IAM records, and
the people who know the system.

92
00:05:15,193 --> 00:05:19,377
It'll be incomplete at first, but we
will make it useful, then improve it

93
00:05:20,377 --> 00:05:23,945
Now that we can see where the
identity has been, we can contain it.

94
00:05:24,711 --> 00:05:28,282
The trap here is to rotate a secret
and assume the problem is solved.

95
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:29,127
It may not be.

96
00:05:29,549 --> 00:05:34,202
An attacker could already have a token,
an active session, or access to the system

97
00:05:34,405 --> 00:05:36,185
that distributes the replacement secret.

98
00:05:36,978 --> 00:05:41,317
We need to stop new access, invalidate
existing access where the architecture

99
00:05:41,317 --> 00:05:45,359
allows it, check that the decision
has reached every enforcement

100
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:49,313
point, and review what happened
before we issue new credentials.

101
00:05:49,749 --> 00:05:52,655
The next slide shows what that
looks like in a payment platform

102
00:05:53,655 --> 00:05:57,788
For this payment API, containment
is a sequence, not a single button.

103
00:05:58,362 --> 00:06:00,992
We stop the workload from
getting new credentials.

104
00:06:01,103 --> 00:06:04,871
We inva-invalidate its
sessions or refresh tokens.

105
00:06:05,393 --> 00:06:07,731
Then we deal with tokens already issued.

106
00:06:08,372 --> 00:06:12,352
With introspection-based tokens,
we can check revocation centrally.

107
00:06:13,344 --> 00:06:18,009
With stateless signed tokens, we
may need a deny list, a credential

108
00:06:18,025 --> 00:06:21,988
version check, or very short validity
combined with gateway enforcement.

109
00:06:23,608 --> 00:06:28,133
We then check every gateway and cache
that could still accept the old token.

110
00:06:28,512 --> 00:06:32,864
Only after that do we review the
ledger calls and issue a replacement

111
00:06:32,876 --> 00:06:35,086
identity with narrower permissions.

112
00:06:35,865 --> 00:06:39,489
A short token lifetime helps,
but it does not cancel a token

113
00:06:39,793 --> 00:06:40,628
that is already out there.

114
00:06:41,628 --> 00:06:45,258
Now that we've stopped the compromised
credential, we need to change how

115
00:06:45,304 --> 00:06:47,026
our services prove who they are.

116
00:06:47,796 --> 00:06:50,890
In this payment example, checkout
shouldn't be trusted just

117
00:06:50,893 --> 00:06:52,467
because it is inside a network.

118
00:06:53,085 --> 00:06:57,476
The payment API needs to prove its
workload identity, and its token

119
00:06:57,477 --> 00:07:00,630
should only work for the right
service and the actions it needs.

120
00:07:01,309 --> 00:07:06,715
A policy engine checks each request, and
an enforcement point applies the decision.

121
00:07:07,696 --> 00:07:11,306
We can manage policy centrally
while spreading enforcement

122
00:07:11,307 --> 00:07:13,085
across gateways or sidecars.

123
00:07:13,565 --> 00:07:17,052
That way, one gateway doesn't become
the next single point of failure

124
00:07:18,052 --> 00:07:19,481
Here's the payment example.

125
00:07:19,537 --> 00:07:23,488
So the payment service identifies
itself as payment API product.

126
00:07:23,989 --> 00:07:27,505
It asks for a token that is
meant for the ledger API, not

127
00:07:27,506 --> 00:07:29,128
for every service in the company.

128
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:34,191
The token carries a limited permission,
such as posting a ledger entry, and

129
00:07:34,192 --> 00:07:35,524
it expires after a short period.

130
00:07:36,518 --> 00:07:40,033
When the ledger receives the request,
its enforcement point checks who

131
00:07:40,039 --> 00:07:44,222
issued the token, which workload
it represents, which API the token

132
00:07:44,224 --> 00:07:48,166
is for, what action is allowed, and
whether the token is still valid.

133
00:07:49,242 --> 00:07:53,026
That is a much stronger basis for trust
than saying this request came from our

134
00:07:53,027 --> 00:07:54,379
internal network, so let it through

135
00:07:55,379 --> 00:07:59,313
Think about a request from checkout
that suddenly looks unusual.

136
00:08:00,028 --> 00:08:02,982
We don't have to choose
between allowing everything and

137
00:08:02,984 --> 00:08:04,647
shutting the whole service down.

138
00:08:04,994 --> 00:08:09,675
We can allow normal access, restrict
the request, make it read-only, ask

139
00:08:09,678 --> 00:08:13,664
the workload to prove itself again,
move it into quarantine or deny it.

140
00:08:14,396 --> 00:08:17,048
These aren't simply six
steps on a risk scale.

141
00:08:17,339 --> 00:08:20,525
The, the right choice depends on
what the service is trying to do.

142
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:23,875
for example, we might block
a suspicious payment, right?

143
00:08:23,957 --> 00:08:26,335
But still allow a safe status check

144
00:08:27,335 --> 00:08:29,837
Let's put those decisions
into the payment flow.

145
00:08:30,762 --> 00:08:36,097
If the workload is verified and
behaving as expected, allow the request.

146
00:08:36,609 --> 00:08:41,198
If something is unusual but not
confirmed as malicious, restrict the

147
00:08:41,198 --> 00:08:42,993
amount or the actions it can perform.

148
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:48,237
If we can safely keep payment status
lookups running, make the service

149
00:08:48,259 --> 00:08:50,401
read-only and block ledger writes.

150
00:08:51,025 --> 00:08:55,341
If the workload needs stronger proof,
ask it to refresh its attestation

151
00:08:55,366 --> 00:08:57,025
or exchange for a new token.

152
00:08:57,784 --> 00:09:02,630
If one instance looks prom-- compromised,
quarantine the, that particular

153
00:09:02,641 --> 00:09:04,500
instance and bring up a clean one.

154
00:09:05,408 --> 00:09:09,042
And if you know the identity is
compromised, deny all the requests for it.

155
00:09:09,793 --> 00:09:14,358
The important part is that a write to
the ledger and a read of payment status

156
00:09:14,596 --> 00:09:19,844
do not have to receive the same decision
So here's what graceful degradation

157
00:09:19,855 --> 00:09:21,963
looks like in our payment example.

158
00:09:22,743 --> 00:09:27,526
Suppose the payment API, starts behaving
in a way we don't trust, we don't keep

159
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:29,898
processing every payment as normal.

160
00:09:30,698 --> 00:09:35,428
We can stop high-risk writes, ask
for strong- stronger proof before

161
00:09:35,428 --> 00:09:40,521
sensitive actions, and keep safe
status checks or read-only requests

162
00:09:41,021 --> 00:09:42,632
available where policy allows.

163
00:09:43,304 --> 00:09:47,293
The service is still useful, but it has
fewer permissions while we investigate.

164
00:09:48,158 --> 00:09:51,941
The priority is to protect sensitive
data movement and keep only the

165
00:09:51,941 --> 00:09:53,816
operations we can still trust running.

166
00:09:54,816 --> 00:09:59,086
Once the immediate risk is under
control, we can start modernizing.

167
00:10:00,156 --> 00:10:03,904
But trying to rebuild every API
during an incident is a good

168
00:10:03,906 --> 00:10:04,995
way to create a second incident.

169
00:10:05,681 --> 00:10:09,651
So the dependency graph helps us
decide what deserves attention first.

170
00:10:10,115 --> 00:10:14,103
Anything directly exposed to the
compromised identity comes first.

171
00:10:14,867 --> 00:10:17,927
Broad permissions and
sensitive data come next.

172
00:10:18,329 --> 00:10:21,414
Long-lived credentials follow
as a medium-term target.

173
00:10:22,310 --> 00:10:26,553
Internal low-privileged services can wait
to be performed as a planned activity.

174
00:10:27,628 --> 00:10:30,944
The gateway gives us a way to
protect the services that are

175
00:10:30,973 --> 00:10:32,653
waiting for a full modernization.

176
00:10:33,066 --> 00:10:36,113
So here's what that priority order
looks like in the payment platform

177
00:10:37,113 --> 00:10:41,620
In this example, we do not start
with the easiest service to rewrite.

178
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:46,264
We start with the payment API
and the ledger write path because

179
00:10:46,292 --> 00:10:49,149
they were directly exposed to the
identity we are investigating.

180
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:55,801
Next, we look at customer profile
and fraud scoring because they have

181
00:10:55,801 --> 00:10:57,567
sensitive data or broad access.

182
00:10:58,290 --> 00:11:01,850
Then we move to the notification
service and its holder credentials.

183
00:11:02,666 --> 00:11:07,106
Internal reporting APIs come later if
their privilege and exposure are low.

184
00:11:07,963 --> 00:11:12,196
The exact order will re-- will
depend on the graph and the business,

185
00:11:12,370 --> 00:11:13,668
but the principle stays the same.

186
00:11:14,368 --> 00:11:19,990
Prioritize by exposure, privilege,
and data sensitivity, not by which

187
00:11:19,990 --> 00:11:21,973
team happens to have spare capacity.

188
00:11:22,973 --> 00:11:27,609
Some of our APIs will be old, that's
understandable, and we may not be

189
00:11:27,609 --> 00:11:29,278
able to change them during recovery.

190
00:11:30,161 --> 00:11:33,659
The gateway gives us a way
to protect those services now

191
00:11:33,701 --> 00:11:35,297
without waiting for a rewrite.

192
00:11:36,266 --> 00:11:38,196
But the gateway needs to
be designed carefully too.

193
00:11:38,896 --> 00:11:43,180
So it should have redundant enforcement
points, and callers must not be able to

194
00:11:43,181 --> 00:11:45,690
skip it and reach the ledger directly.

195
00:11:46,534 --> 00:11:51,029
We also need to direct, or decide
what happens if the policy engine

196
00:11:51,358 --> 00:11:52,709
is temporarily unavailable.

197
00:11:53,384 --> 00:11:57,785
How long can a cache decision be used,
and what emergency access is allowed?

198
00:11:58,662 --> 00:12:01,522
Those safeguards are part
of the architecture, and it

199
00:12:01,585 --> 00:12:02,580
should not be an afterthought.

200
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:05,514
So let's follow one payment request.

201
00:12:05,905 --> 00:12:09,337
So the checkout service sends a
request with its workload token.

202
00:12:09,902 --> 00:12:12,008
The gateway is the
policy enforcement point.

203
00:12:12,111 --> 00:12:15,050
It intercepts the request
and checks the token claims.

204
00:12:15,556 --> 00:12:19,288
It asks the policy decision point
whether this caller can perform

205
00:12:19,289 --> 00:12:20,922
this action in the current context.

206
00:12:21,462 --> 00:12:24,333
The decision comes back and the
enforcement point applies it.

207
00:12:25,184 --> 00:12:29,272
If the posit-- if the policy allows
the ledger write, the request

208
00:12:29,272 --> 00:12:31,454
reaches the existing ledger API.

209
00:12:32,069 --> 00:12:35,462
If it is read-only or denied,
the gateway stops the write

210
00:12:35,538 --> 00:12:36,830
before it reaches the service.

211
00:12:37,612 --> 00:12:41,092
The request and decision are sent
to the audit platform either way.

212
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:45,593
That separation between decision and
enforcement is what makes the flow easier

213
00:12:45,610 --> 00:12:51,187
to scale and operate So how do we know
the recovery is actually getting safer?

214
00:12:52,134 --> 00:12:55,664
so these first four measures
give us a clear picture.

215
00:12:55,813 --> 00:12:59,298
We count the trust paths we've
removed from the dependency graph.

216
00:12:59,917 --> 00:13:04,058
We time how long it takes before
every affected enforcement point

217
00:13:04,135 --> 00:13:05,855
rejects the compromised identity.

218
00:13:06,717 --> 00:13:10,864
We check how many service calls now
use scoped short-lived credentials,

219
00:13:11,432 --> 00:13:14,863
and we track how much of the
high-risk API surface is covered

220
00:13:14,880 --> 00:13:16,565
by policy enforcements and logging.

221
00:13:17,364 --> 00:13:21,241
In our example, revoking the
payment API credential isn't enough

222
00:13:21,266 --> 00:13:24,837
if the ledger or fraud service
still accepts an older access path

223
00:13:25,837 --> 00:13:29,434
The last three measures tells, tells
us whether the new controls are

224
00:13:29,445 --> 00:13:33,383
helping and whether we can improve,
or we can prove what happened.

225
00:13:34,548 --> 00:13:38,284
We look at which authorization
outcomes are being used, how long

226
00:13:38,296 --> 00:13:42,516
restrictions last, and whether payment
success or false alarms are affected.

227
00:13:43,277 --> 00:13:47,603
Then we track how many of our highest
risk APIs meet the security baseline.

228
00:13:48,382 --> 00:13:51,890
Finally, we check whether each
request leaves a useful record.

229
00:13:52,299 --> 00:13:55,904
Who called, what they wanted
to do, what decision we made,

230
00:13:56,032 --> 00:13:57,449
and which policy was applied.

231
00:13:58,037 --> 00:14:01,363
We want useful evidence, not
just a dashboard full of numbers.

232
00:14:02,363 --> 00:14:04,760
So this is what visibility
means in practice.

233
00:14:04,819 --> 00:14:08,297
We should be able to follow one
payment request from checkout to the

234
00:14:08,297 --> 00:14:10,133
payment API and then to the ledger.

235
00:14:10,886 --> 00:14:15,480
At each step, we want to know which
workload identity made the call, what

236
00:14:15,507 --> 00:14:20,512
decision the gateway made, which policy
version was used, and the trace ID

237
00:14:20,556 --> 00:14:22,468
that ties all the events together.

238
00:14:23,342 --> 00:14:26,682
Then we can confirm that the old
credential is rejected and that

239
00:14:26,682 --> 00:14:28,285
safe operations are still available.

240
00:14:29,005 --> 00:14:33,062
Without that information, we are
guessing, and with it, the recovery

241
00:14:33,063 --> 00:14:36,785
team can make decisions based on
what the system is actually doing

242
00:14:37,785 --> 00:14:43,802
So the recovery sequence becomes incident,
contain and map, harden and instrument,

243
00:14:43,977 --> 00:14:46,492
validate, and then restore in stages.

244
00:14:47,326 --> 00:14:52,117
The key change is that the validation
happens before normal trust comes back.

245
00:14:52,630 --> 00:14:55,548
We do not just check
that the service starts.

246
00:14:56,459 --> 00:15:01,098
We check that the old credential no
longer works and the new identity is

247
00:15:01,099 --> 00:15:04,671
scoped correctly, and that the safe
parts of the service still function.

248
00:15:05,577 --> 00:15:08,689
That brings us directly to a
controlled chaos experiment.

249
00:15:09,140 --> 00:15:13,364
We can test the assumptions before we put
normal traffic back through the system.

250
00:15:14,364 --> 00:15:18,406
Here is one experiment the team
could run in an isolated environment.

251
00:15:19,417 --> 00:15:25,313
So our hypothesis is that revoking
payment API prod will close the ledger

252
00:15:25,313 --> 00:15:29,701
path at every enforcement point, while
safe status reads remain available.

253
00:15:30,444 --> 00:15:34,145
We disable the test identity,
then deliberately replay a token

254
00:15:34,154 --> 00:15:35,705
that was issued before revocation.

255
00:15:36,421 --> 00:15:40,954
We watch the gateways, cache, policy
decisions, ledger writes, read

256
00:15:40,961 --> 00:15:42,640
availability, and audit events.

257
00:15:43,219 --> 00:15:47,531
We pass only if the old token is
rejected within the agreed target.

258
00:15:48,271 --> 00:15:52,447
There is no route around the gateway,
and the safe read path still works.

259
00:15:53,329 --> 00:15:56,647
Then we repeat with the policy
decision point unavailable.

260
00:15:57,750 --> 00:16:02,349
That will tell us whether the documented
cache and fail-safe behavior really works.

261
00:16:02,980 --> 00:16:06,171
We use the incident to design
the experiment, but we run the

262
00:16:06,172 --> 00:16:10,812
experiment safely and repeatably
and, not against a live attack

263
00:16:11,812 --> 00:16:14,330
So there are a few fair, objections.

264
00:16:14,778 --> 00:16:18,984
The business may say it needs the
payment service, back immediately.

265
00:16:19,556 --> 00:16:21,542
That is why graded access matters.

266
00:16:21,717 --> 00:16:25,093
Bring back the safe operations
first, and then restore full

267
00:16:25,161 --> 00:16:26,892
trust as evidence supports it.

268
00:16:27,595 --> 00:16:31,631
Teams may say they cannot rewrite old
APIs in the middle of an incident.

269
00:16:32,189 --> 00:16:33,100
They do not have to.

270
00:16:33,622 --> 00:16:36,815
Protect them at the gateway
and modernize later.

271
00:16:37,384 --> 00:16:41,535
And many organizations will say that
they do not have a dependency map.

272
00:16:41,932 --> 00:16:45,655
So start with what you can see and
the people who know the system.

273
00:16:46,207 --> 00:16:48,146
The first graph can be provisional.

274
00:16:48,412 --> 00:16:52,406
And one more thing, the policy engine
and the gateway must be resilient too.

275
00:16:53,483 --> 00:16:58,196
Distributed enforcement, clear cache
limits, emergency access, and protection

276
00:16:58,198 --> 00:17:04,672
against bypass are part of the design and
not optional extras If you remember a few

277
00:17:04,721 --> 00:17:06,914
things from this talk, make them these.

278
00:17:07,095 --> 00:17:09,972
A restore is not automatically a recovery.

279
00:17:10,569 --> 00:17:13,449
map the dependencies before
you bring everything back.

280
00:17:13,837 --> 00:17:17,523
Remove compromised access rather
than simply changing a secret.

281
00:17:18,114 --> 00:17:21,145
Give workloads identities
that can be verified.

282
00:17:21,663 --> 00:17:25,406
When it is safe, reduce permissions
instead of forcing every request

283
00:17:25,428 --> 00:17:26,796
into all or nothing behavior.

284
00:17:27,723 --> 00:17:31,859
Protect legacy APIs by means of a gateway
while you modernize in risk order.

285
00:17:32,739 --> 00:17:35,788
And measure the controls, then
test them under control failure.

286
00:17:36,597 --> 00:17:39,564
The payment example is just
one way to see the idea.

287
00:17:39,719 --> 00:17:43,870
The same approach applies anywhere
a compromised service identity

288
00:17:44,079 --> 00:17:45,914
can move through an API ecosystem.

289
00:17:46,914 --> 00:17:50,899
So every incident gives us
evidence about a path we did not

290
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:52,914
understand or control well enough.

291
00:17:53,652 --> 00:17:58,175
We should use that incident, we should use
that evidence not by changing everything

292
00:17:58,224 --> 00:18:03,568
at once and not by delaying, delaying
recovery forever, but by removing the

293
00:18:03,573 --> 00:18:06,304
access paths that made the incident worse.

294
00:18:07,003 --> 00:18:11,581
The NIST publications on this slide
provide important foundations.

295
00:18:11,813 --> 00:18:15,582
The five-step sequence is a
practical way to connect those

296
00:18:15,651 --> 00:18:17,274
foundations to API recovery.

297
00:18:17,922 --> 00:18:20,461
The aim is not simply to
get back to where we were.

298
00:18:20,859 --> 00:18:25,334
It is to make the next recovery safer
than the last one So if there is

299
00:18:25,338 --> 00:18:30,118
one line I hope you take away, it is
this: Don't just restore what broke.

300
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:31,998
Rebuild it stronger.

301
00:18:32,442 --> 00:18:34,384
Every recovery is a chance to harden.

302
00:18:35,238 --> 00:18:39,199
I am Praveen Kumar Gopalakrishnan,
and, this is where I'll sign off.

303
00:18:39,668 --> 00:18:43,613
Thank you for watching, and a big
thank you to Conf42 for giving me

304
00:18:43,613 --> 00:18:45,586
this opportunity to share my thoughts.

305
00:18:45,859 --> 00:18:46,693
It's been a pleasure.

306
00:18:47,074 --> 00:18:48,356
Thank you and take care.
